## 1. DROP TABLE

DROP is a DDL command used to permanently remove a table
along with its data and structure.


DROP TABLE copy2;


Explanation:
The table and its data are completely deleted.


==================================================
2. DROP MULTIPLE TABLES
==================================================

DROP TABLE pyemployee1, pyemployee3, pyemployee5;


Explanation:
Deletes multiple tables at once.


==================================================
3. DROP TABLE IF EXISTS
==================================================

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS pyemployee1, pyemployee3, pyemployee5;


Explanation:
Drops the tables only if they exist.


==================================================
4. DROP vs TRUNCATE
==================================================

TRUNCATE
→ Removes all rows.
→ Table structure remains.
→ Resets AUTO_INCREMENT.
→ WHERE is not allowed.

DROP
→ Removes the complete table.
→ Data and structure are deleted.
→ AUTO_INCREMENT is also removed.
→ WHERE is not allowed.


==================================================
5. ALTER TABLE
==================================================

ALTER is a DDL command used to modify the structure
of an existing table.


Syntax:

ALTER TABLE table_name operation;


==================================================
6. ADD COLUMN
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
ADD COLUMN email VARCHAR(20);


Explanation:
Adds a new column at the end.


==================================================
7. ADD MULTIPLE COLUMNS
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
ADD COLUMN mobile VARCHAR(20),
ADD COLUMN course VARCHAR(30),
ADD COLUMN salary DECIMAL(10,2);


Explanation:
Adds multiple columns at once.


==================================================
8. ADD COLUMN FIRST
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
ADD COLUMN gender VARCHAR(10) FIRST;


Explanation:
Adds the column at the beginning.


==================================================
9. ADD COLUMN AFTER
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
ADD COLUMN dob DATE AFTER name;


Explanation:
Adds the column after the specified column.


==================================================
10. DROP COLUMN
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
DROP COLUMN dob;


Explanation:
Permanently removes a column.


==================================================
11. DROP MULTIPLE COLUMNS
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
DROP COLUMN mobile,
DROP COLUMN course;


Explanation:
Removes multiple columns.


==================================================
12. MODIFY COLUMN
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
MODIFY COLUMN age SMALLINT;


Explanation:
Changes the column definition.


Example:

ALTER TABLE azadi
MODIFY COLUMN name VARCHAR(30);


==================================================
13. MODIFY WITH CONSTRAINT
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
MODIFY COLUMN name VARCHAR(30) NOT NULL;


Explanation:
Changes datatype and constraint.


==================================================
14. MODIFY DEFAULT VALUE
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
MODIFY COLUMN salary DECIMAL(10,2) DEFAULT 1000;


Explanation:
Sets a default value for the column.


==================================================
15. CHANGE COLUMN
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
CHANGE COLUMN name full_name VARCHAR(30);


Explanation:
Renames a column and can also change its definition.


==================================================
16. RENAME COLUMN
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
RENAME COLUMN name TO full_name;


Explanation:
Renames only the column.


==================================================
17. RENAME TABLE USING ALTER
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadi
RENAME TO hamariazadi;


Explanation:
Renames the table.


==================================================
18. RENAME TABLE
==================================================

RENAME TABLE hamariazadi TO sabkiazadi;


Explanation:
Renames the table.


==================================================
19. MODIFY vs CHANGE
==================================================

MODIFY
→ Changes column definition.
→ Column name remains same.

CHANGE
→ Can rename the column.
→ Can also change its definition.


==================================================
20. ADD PRIMARY KEY
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadstudent
ADD PRIMARY KEY(id);


Explanation:
Adds a primary key to an existing table.


==================================================
21. DROP PRIMARY KEY
==================================================

ALTER TABLE azadstudent
DROP PRIMARY KEY;


Explanation:
Removes the primary key.


==================================================
22. IMPORTANT: AUTO_INCREMENT + PRIMARY KEY
==================================================

If a column has AUTO_INCREMENT, it must have an index.

Therefore, an AUTO_INCREMENT column cannot simply lose
its only index.


==================================================
23. HOMEWORK PRACTICAL
==================================================

Create a table with:

id
name
salary


Insert 5 records.

Then add PRIMARY KEY and AUTO_INCREMENT using ALTER.


Example:

CREATE TABLE pyemp(
    id INT,
    name VARCHAR(20),
    salary DECIMAL(10,2)
);


Insert 5 records:

INSERT INTO pyemp
VALUES
(1,"Anil",99999),
(2,"Abhi",99889),
(3,"Harsh",99889),
(4,"Bhumii",99777),
(5,"Kuldeep",99666);


Add PRIMARY KEY:

ALTER TABLE pyemp
ADD PRIMARY KEY(id);


Add AUTO_INCREMENT:

ALTER TABLE pyemp
MODIFY COLUMN id INT AUTO_INCREMENT;


Now insert without ID:

INSERT INTO pyemp(name,salary)
VALUES("Raj",99000);


The ID will be:

6


Because the next AUTO_INCREMENT value is generated
automatically.


==================================================
QUICK REVISION
==================================================

DROP
→ Deletes table completely.

TRUNCATE
→ Deletes all rows.

ALTER
→ Changes table structure.

ADD
→ Adds column.

DROP COLUMN
→ Removes column.

MODIFY
→ Changes column definition.

CHANGE
→ Renames + changes definition.

RENAME
→ Changes table/column name.

ADD PRIMARY KEY
→ Adds primary key.

DROP PRIMARY KEY
→ Removes primary key.

AUTO_INCREMENT
→ Automatically generates the next ID.